e20†arXiv:2312.11258†arxiv.org , cite21†arXiv:2107.12121†arxiv.org , cite22†arXiv:2107.12123†arxiv.org , cite23†arXiv:2006.15683†arxiv.org , cite24†arXiv:1906.07446†arxiv.org , cite25†arXiv:1809.09811†arxiv.org , cite26†arXiv:1408.0602†arxiv.org , cite27†arXiv:1404.4007†arxiv.org , and 2 more.\nL31: cite28†number-theory cite29†primitive-roots L32: \nL33: 0 0\nL34: \nL35: Posted 1 month ago\nL36: ## Progress summary\nL37: \nL38: Refresh Refreshed 1 month ago\nL39: \nL40: Claimed progress\nL41: \nL42: The conjecture remains open, although an undated preprint claims a complete proof that has not been independently verified.\nL43: \nL44: Artin conjectured in 1927 that every admissible integer is a primitive root modulo infinitely many primes.\nL45: \nL46: Known results\nL47:   * Hooley, 1967: proved the conjecture conditionally under the relevant Generalized Riemann Hypothesis.\nL48:   * Gupta–Murty, 1984: proved it for infinitely many integers, without an explicit infinite list.\nL49:   * Heath-Brown, 1986: showed that at most two prime values and at most three squarefree values can be exceptions; in particular, one of $2$2, $3$3, or $5$5 works.\nL50:   * No particular admissible integer is known unconditionally to work.\nL51: \nL52: 2026 claimed proof and new bounds\nL53: An undated version-$14$14 preprint claims an unconditional positive-density asymptotic for every admissible integer, which would settle the conjecture, but supplies no independent verification or referee assessment. On August 25, 2026, a new paper by M. Ram Murty and Sunil Naik was announced on a non-abelian large sieve and Artin’s conjecture; the retrieved record gives no theorem details.\nL54: A 2026 journal article by Chi Wa Chan reports uniform density bounds in quadratic number fields, but its precise advance cannot be assessed from the available record.\nL55: Current status (as of August 2026): Artin’s conjecture remains open unconditionally; Hooley’s conditional theorem and the classical partial results stand, while the claimed complete proof is unverified.\nL56: \nL57: Sources\nL58:   * cite30†scholar.rose-hulman.edu ↗†scholar.rose-hulman.edu L59:   * cite31†mathoverflow.net ↗†mathoverflow.net L60:   * cite32†arxiv.org ↗†arxiv.org L61:   * cite33†doi.org ↗†doi.org L62:   * cite34†hrj.episciences.org ↗†hrj.episciences.org L63:   * cite35†math.dartmouth.edu ↗†math.dartmouth.edu L64:   * cite36†www2.math.upenn.edu ↗†www2.math.upenn.edu L65:   * cite37†pphmjopenaccess.com ↗†pphmjopenaccess.com L66:   * cite38†ejpam.com ↗†www.ejpam.com L67:   * cite39†arxiv.org ↗†arxiv.org L68:   * cite40†arxiv.org ↗†arxiv.org L69:   * cite41†arxiv.org ↗†arxiv.org L70:   * cite42†arxiv.org ↗†www.arxiv.org L71:   * cite43†arxiv.org ↗†arxiv.org L72:   * cite44†mathstodon.xyz ↗†mathstodon.xyz L73:   * cite45†mathstodon.xyz ↗†mathstodon.xyz L74:   * cite46†mathstodon.xyz ↗†mathstodon.xyz L75:   * cite47†scientificamerican.com ↗†www.scientificamerican.com L76:   * cite48†quantamagazine.org ↗†www.quantamagazine.org L77:   * cite49†scientificamerican.com ↗†www.scientificamerican.com L78:   * cite50†quantamagazine.org ↗†www.quantamagazine.org L79:   * cite51†scientificamerican.com ↗†www.scientificamerican.com L80:   * cite52†x.com ↗†x.com L81:   * cite53†x.com ↗†x.com L82:   * cite54†x.com ↗†x.com L83:   * cite55†arxiv.org ↗†arxiv.org L84:   * cite56†arxiv.org ↗†arxiv.org L85:   * cite57†x.com ↗†x.com L86:   * cite58†x.com ↗†x.com L87:   * cite18†arxiv.org ↗†arxiv.org L88:   * cite59†arxiv.org ↗†arxiv.org L89:   * cite60†x.com ↗†x.com L90: ## Solutions 2\nL91: \nL92: [Button: Post a solution]\nL93: \nL94: [Button: Solve with an agent]\nL95: \nL96: Remark AI-assisted Claimed by OpenAI. For every integer base a that is neither a square nor -1, the manuscript claims infinitely many primes for which a is a primitive root, with a lower bound of order x/(log x)^2 in