ditional-lower-bound-prime-bases.pdf†github.com L114: \nL115:   * OpenAI-029-02-Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases.pdf 450,863 bytescite66†Open L116: \nL117: Endorse 0\nL118: \nL119: 0 0\nL120: \nL121: cite64†Shaowu Zhang Posted 23 hours ago · edited 23 hours ago\nL122: ## Recently Viewed\nL123: \nL124: Problems you open will appear here.\nL125: \nL126: ## Following\nL127: \nL128: Loading…\nL129: \nL130: cite12†About cite67†Support cite68†Privacy cite69†Terms --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLarge prime factors of well-distributed sequences (https://arxiv.org/html/2402.11884v4)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2402.11884v4\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 530\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract. L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:     1. cite7†1.1 Background L18:     2. cite8†1.2 Some well-distributed arithmetic sequences L19:     3. cite9†1.3 Main results L20:     4. cite10†1.4 Acknowledgements L21:   3. cite11†2 Resemblance to Poisson-Dirichlet: a proof of Theorem and Lemma L22:   4. cite12†3 Upper bounds on largest primes: a proof of Theorem L23:   5. cite13†References L24: \nL25: cite14†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L26: \nL27: arXiv:2402.11884v4 [math.NT] 09 Apr 2026\nL28: # Large prime factors of well-distributed sequences\nL29: \nL30: Abhishek Bharadwaj, Brad Rodgers\nL31: ###### Abstract.\nL32: We study the distribution of large prime factors of a random element $u$ of arithmetic sequences satisfying simple regularity and equidistribution properties. We show that if such an arithmetic sequence has level of distribution $1$ the large prime factors of $u$ tend to a Poisson-Dirichlet process, while if the sequence has any positive level of distribution the correlation functions of large prime factors tend to a Poisson-Dirichlet process against test functions of restricted support.\nL33: For sequences with positive level of distribution, we also estimate the probability the largest prime factor of $u$ is greater than $u^{1-\\epsilon}$, showing that this probability is $O(\\epsilon)$.\nL34: Examples of sequences described include shifted primes and values of single-variable irreducible polynomials.\nL35: \nL36: The proofs involve (i) a characterization of the Poisson-Dirichlet process due to Arratia-Kochman-Miller and (ii) an upper bound sieve.\nL37: ## 1. Introduction\nL38: ### 1.1. Background\nL39: \nL40: The purpose of this note is to study the distribution of large prime factors of elements in sequences which satisfy only a few minimal conditions.\nL41: \nL42: Let us recall the classical theory and notation: let $P^{+}(u)$ be the largest prime factor of a positive integer $u$. It is a consequence of a result of Dickman [cite15†13 ] that for any fixed $c\\in(0,\\infty)$,\nL43: \nL44:  | $$\\frac{1}{x}|\\{u\\leq x:\\,P^{+}(u)\\leq u^{c}\\}|\\sim\\rho(1/c),$$  |\nL45: as $x\\rightarrow\\infty$, where $\\rho:(0,\\infty)\\rightarrow(0,1]$ is a continuous function called the Dickman function. (See e.g. [cite16†34 , Ch. 7.1] for a modern account.)\nL46: This result was generalized and given a probabilistic interpretation by Billingsley [cite17†4 ] (and independently Knuth and Trabb Pardo [cite18†30 ] and Vershik [cite19†43 ]). Let $u$ be chosen randomly and uniformly from the integers from $1$ to $x$. Then the above result says that $\\log P^{+}(u)/\\log u$ tends in distribution to a nonnegative random variable $L_{1}$ with cumulative distribution function $\\mathbb{P}(L_{1}\\leq c)=\\rho(1/c)$.\nL47: Moreover, let $p_{1}\\geq p_{2}\\geq\\cdots$ be the prime factors of $u$ listed with multiplicity, with the convention that $p_{j}=1$ if $u$ has fewer than $j$ prime factors (so that $p_{1}=P^{+}(u)$ and $u=p_{1}p_{2}\\cdots$). Billingsley showed that there is a sequence of (dependent) random variables $L_{1},L_{2},...$ such that for any $k\\geq 1$, and any fixed constants $c_{1},c_{2},...,c_{k}\\in[0,\\infty)$,\nL48:  | $$\\mathbb{P}\\Big(\\frac{\\log p_{1}}{\\log u}\\leq c_{1},...,\\frac{\\log p_{k}}{\\log u}\\leq c_{k}\\Big)\\rightarrow\\mathbb{P}(L_{1}\\leq c_{1},...,L_{k}\\leq c_{k}),$$  |\nL49: \nL50: as $x\\rightarrow\\infty$. That is, the process $\\tfrac{\\log p_{1}}{\\log u},\\tfrac{\\log p_{2}}{\\log u},...$ tends in distribution to $L_{1},L_{2},...$. (See e.g. [cite20†5 ] for a modern probabilistic account. Here and in what follows we discard with the case $u=1$ by adopting the formal convention $\\log 1/\\log 1=1$.)\nL51: For each $k$ an explicit formula for $\\mathbb{P}(L_{1}\\leq c_{1},...,L_{k}\\leq c_{k})$ can be written down (see [cite20†5 , Thm. 4.4] for a density formula), but the formula is somewhat complicated. The following characterization is simpler: let $U_{1},U_{2},...$ be independent and identically distributed uniform random variables on the interval $(0,1)$, and define random variables $G_{1}=1-U_{1}$, $G_{2}=U_{1}(1-U_{2})$, $G_{3}=U_{1}U_{2}(1-U_{3})$, … .\nL52: Then $L_{1}\\geq L_{2}\\geq...$ may be defined as the outcome of sorting $G_{1},G_{2},...$ into nonincreasing order.\nL53: The sequence of random variables $L_{1},L_{2},...$ is known as the Poisson-Dirichlet process with parameter $\\theta=1$. As the name suggests there are Poisson-Dirichlet processes with $\\theta\\neq 1$, but in this paper we will only deal with $\\theta=1$, and so if there is no risk of confusion, we refer to $L_{1},L_{2},...$ as simply the Poisson-Dirichlet process. (See [cite20†5 , cite21†29 ] for an account of general $\\theta$, along with a more detailed introduction to the case $\\theta=1$.)\nL54: We note that $\\tfrac{\\log p_{1}}{\\log u}+\\tfrac{\\log p_{2}}{\\log u}+\\cdots=1$, and likewise $L_{1}+L_{2}+\\cdots=1$ almost surely.\nL55: ### 1.2. Some well-distributed arithmetic sequences\nL56: It is natural to wonder whether this statistical pattern governing the distribution of large prime factors of random integers extends to other arithmetic sequences.\nL57: Some cases which have been studied extensively include shifted primes [cite22†2 , cite23†16 , cite24†18 , cite25†21 , cite26†25 ] – that is, the sequence $\\{p-a\\}$ for a constant $a$ where $p$ ranges over the primes – and the values of irreducible polynomials [cite27†7 , cite28†9 , cite29†8 , cite30†10 , cite31†11 , cite32†12 , cite33†23 , cite34†24 , cite35†27 , cite36†33 ] – that is the sequence $\\{F(n)\\}$, where $F$ is an irreducible polynomial with integer coefficients and a positive leading coefficient and $n$ ranges over the integers.\nL58: In this paper we study a quite general class of arithmetic sequences. Let $(a_{n})$ be a sequence of nonnegative numbers. Define the quantities\nL59: \nL60:  | $$N(x)=\\sum_{n\\leq x}a_{n},\\quad\\quad N_{d}(x)=\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}n\\leq x\\\\\nL61: n\\equiv 0\\ (\\mathrm{mod}\\ d)\\end{subarray}}a_{n}.$$  |  | (1)\nL62: \nL63: We say that the sequence $(a_{n})$:\nL64: \nL65:   1. (A)\nL66: \nL67: Has index $\\alpha$ if for some $\\alpha>0$,\nL68: \nL69:  | $$N(x)=x^{\\alpha+o(1)},$$  |\nL70: \nL71:   2. (B)\nL72: \nL73: Has a level of distribution $\\vartheta$ if for any $0<c<\\vartheta$ and any $A>0$,\nL74:  | $$\\sum_{d\\leq x^{c}}|N_{d}(x)-g(d)N(x)|\\ll_{c,A}\\frac{1}{(\\log x)^{A}}N(x),$$  |  | (2)\nL75: \nL76: where $g(d)$ is a multiplicative function with $g(d)\\in[0,1]$ for all $d\\geq 1$, and\nL77: \nL78:  | $$\\sum_{p\\leq x}g(p)\\log p=\\log x+O(1),\\quad\\quad g(d)=O(C^{\\Omega(d)}/d),$$  |  | (3)\nL79: \nL80: for some constant $C\\geq 1$.\nL81: \nL82:   3. (C)\nL83: \nL84: Is congruence uniform if there are constants $B\\geq 0$, $C\\geq 1$ such that\nL85: \nL86:  | $$N_{d}(x)\\ll\\Big(\\frac{C^{\\Omega(d)}}{d}N(x)+C^{\\Omega(d)}\\Big)(\\log x)^{B},\\quad\\textrm{for}\\;d\\leq x.$$  |\nL87: Additionally we say the sequence $(a_{n})$:\nL88: \nL89:   * •\nL90: \nL91: Is $\\sigma$ well-distributed if $(a_{n})$ satisfies each of (A), (B), and (C) and $\\sigma$ is any positive number less than or equal to $\\alpha$ and $\\vartheta$.\nL92: \nL93: Here as usual $\\Omega(d)$ is the number of prime factors of $d$ counted with multiplicity. Note that (cite37†3 ) implies that $g(p^{k})<1$ except for finitely many primes $p$.\nL94: Likewise note that we trivially have $N_{d}(x)\\leq N(x)$, so that the condition for congruence uniformity is only meaningful for those integers $d$ with prime factors $p>C$.\nL95: \nL96: It will typically be the case that $a_{n}$ is the indicator function of $n$ belonging to some subset of the integers, and in that case we will also describe that subset by the above terminology as long as there is no chance for confusion.\nL97: ###### Remark 1.\nL98: \nL99: Let us explain some intuition for these conditions. (A) is self-explanatory; in (B) one may keep in mind the examples $g(d)=1/d$ or $g(d)=1/\\phi(d)$; and (C) may be thought of as a more technical condition – the reader may check that it is trivially satisfied if the sequence $(a_{n})$ is bounded and $N(x)\\gg x/(\\log x)^{B}$ for some $B$.\nL100: Examples of sequences satisfying these conditions are described by the following propositions. Throughout the paper, for a proposition $\\mathfrak{A}$, we use the notation $\\mathbf{1}[\\mathfrak{A}]$ to be $1$ if $\\mathfrak{A}$ is true and $0$ otherwise.\nL101: ###### Proposition 2.\nL102: \nL103: Shifted primes are $1/2$ well-distributed. That is: for a fixed integer $a$, consider the set $\\mathcal{B}=\\{p-a:\\,p\\;\\textrm{is prime}\\}$ and let $a_{n}=\\mathbf{1}[n\\in\\mathcal{B}]$. Then $(a_{n})$ has index $1$, has level of distribution $\\vartheta=1/2$, and is congruence uniform.\nL104: ###### Proof.\nL105: \nL106: That the sequence has index $\\alpha=1$ follows from the prime number theorem (or indeed Chebyshev’s bounds). By Remark cite38†1 , congruence uniformity also follows from Chebyshev’s lower bound $\\pi(x)\\gg x/\\log x$. The level of distribution $1/2$ follows from the Bombieri-Vinogradov theorem [cite39†6 , Thm. 9.2.1], with $g(d)=\\mathbf{1}_{(a,d)=1}/\\phi(d)$, where $\\mathbf{1}_{(a,d)=1}$ is $1$ when $d$ is coprime to $a$ and $0$ otherwise. ∎\nL107: ###### Proposition 3.\nL108: \nL109: The values of irreducible polynomials of degree $D$ are $1/D$ well-distributed. That is: for a polynomial $F(X)\\in\\mathbb{Z}[X]$ of degree $D\\geq 1$ which is irreducible with positive leading coefficient, consider the set $\\mathcal{C}=\\{F(n):\\,n\\in\\mathbb{N}_{\\geq 1}\\}\\cap\\mathbb{N}_{\\geq 1}$ and let $a_{n}=\\mathbf{1}[n\\in\\mathcal{C}]$. Then $(a_{n})$ has index $1/D$, has level of distribution $\\vartheta=1/D$, and is congruence uniform.\nL110: ###### Proof.\nL111: \nL112: That the index is $1/D$ follows from the fact that $N(x)\\asymp x^{1/D}$.\nL113: \nL114: We see that the level of distribution is $1/D$ in the following way. For a natural number $d$, let $h(d)$ be the number of distinct roots of $F$ modulo $d$. By the Chinese Remainder Theorem (see [cite40†37 , Theorem 46]) we note that $h$ is multiplicative. Set $g(d)=h(d)/d$ and note $g$ is multiplicative also.\nL115: Obviously $g(d)\\in[0,1]$. We will show that (cite41†2 ) and (cite37†3 ) are satisfied for this function $g$. Let us show that (cite37†3 ) holds first. It is known that $\\sum_{p\\leq x}g(p)\\log p=\\log x+O(1)$; this is just a weak claim that $F$ will on average have one root modulo a prime, see e.g. [cite42†36 , Eq. (4), Pg. 352], or [cite43†31 , Corollary 4] for a more modern statement.\nL116: The second claim in (cite37†3 ) is just the claim that $h(d)\\ll C^{\\Omega(d)}$ for some constant $C>1$. This follows from the multiplicativity of $h$ and [cite40†37 , Theorem 54] by taking $C=D\\cdot\\mathrm{disc}(F)^{2}$.\nL117: \nL118: Now, let us now prove (cite41†2 ). We first establish that\nL119: \nL120:  | $$|N_{d}(x)-g(d)N(x)|\\ll h(d),$$  |  | (4)\nL121: \nL122: where the implicit constant depends only on $F$.\nL123: To see this, note that since $F(n+d)\\equiv F(n)\\bmod d$, in every interval of length $d$ we will have $F(n)\\equiv 0\\ (\\mathrm{mod}\\ d)$ for $h(d)$ values of $n$. In particular since $F$ is eventually increasing, there is some sufficiently large $x_{0}$ (depending on $F$) such that whenever $N(x)$ increases by $d$ on an interval to the right of $x_{0}$, $N_{d}(x)$ will increase by $h(d)$ on the same interval. Therefore\nL124: \nL125:  | $$|N_{d}(x)-g(d)N(x)|\\leq h(d)+|N_{d}(x_{0})-g(d)N(x_{0})|=h(d)+O(1)$$  |\nL126: with the $O(1)$ term present to account for behavior of this quantity for $x\\leq x_{0}$.\nL127: \nL128: If $h(d)\\geq 1$, this verifies (cite44†4 ). On the other hand if $h(d)=0$, we have $N_{d}(x)=g(d)N(x)=0$ for all $x$, so (cite44†4 ) is verified in this case also.\nL129: \nL130: Hence from (cite44†4 ),\nL131: \nL132:  | $$\\sum_{d\\leq x^{c}}|N_{d}(x)-g(d)N(x)|\\ll\\sum_{d\\leq x^{c}}h(d)\\ll_{c}x^{c}(\\log x)^{A},$$  |  | (5)\nL133: where we use Lemma cite45†4 , proved below, in the last estimate. As $N(x)\\asymp x^{1/D}$, we see (cite41†2 ) is satisfied as long as $c<1/D$.\nL134: \nL135: To prove congruence uniformity, we use the bound $N_{d}(x)=g(d)N(x)+O(h(d))$. Consequently for a constant $C>1$ as above, we see that $N_{d}(x)\\ll\\frac{C^{\\Omega(d)}}{d}N(x)+C^{\\Omega(d)}$, for all $d\\leq x$. ∎\nL136: \nL137: We have used one of the following results above and we will need them later as well.\nL138: ###### Lemma 4.\nL139: \nL140: Suppose $h(d)$ is a multiplicative function and $g(d)=h(d)/d$ satisfies $g(d)\\in[0,1]$ for all $d$ and $g(d)=O(C^{\\Omega(d)}/d)$ for some constant $C\\geq 1$. Then for some constant $A>0$,\nL141: \nL142:  | $$\\sum_{n\\leq x}g(n)\\ll(\\log x)^{A}$$  |\nL143: \nL144: and\nL145: \nL146:  | $$\\sum_{n\\leq x}h(n)\\ll x(\\log x)^{A}.$$  |\nL147: ###### Proof.\nL148: \nL149: Let $p_{1},...,p_{\\ell}$ be the finite set of primes less than $2C$, and set $P=p_{1}\\cdots p_{\\ell}$. Then\nL150: \nL151:  | $$\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}n\\leq x\\\\\nL152: (n,P)=1\\end{subarray}}g(n)\\ll\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}n\\leq x\\\\\nL153: (n,P)=1\\end{subarray}}C^{\\Omega(n)}/n\\leq\\prod_{\\begin{subarray}{c}p\\leq x\\\\\nL154: (p,P)=1\\end{subarray}}\\Big(1+\\frac{C}{p}+\\frac{C^{2}}{p^{2}}+\\cdots\\Big)\\\\\nL155: =\\exp\\Big(\\sum_{p\\leq x}\\frac{C}{p}+O(1)\\Big)\\ll(\\log x)^{C},$$  |\nL156: where we have used the fact that $C/p$ in the product above is no more than $1/2$ in order to sum the series. Using multiplicativity and $g(p_{i}^{e_{i}})\\in[0,1]$ for $1\\leq i\\leq\\ell$, we have from a crude bound\nL157:  | $$\\sum_{n\\leq x}g(n)\\leq\\sum_{p_{1}^{e_{1}}\\cdots p_{\\ell}^{e_{\\ell}}\\leq x}\\;\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}m\\leq x/p_{1}^{e_{1}}\\cdots p_{\\ell}^{e_{\\ell}}\\\\\nL158: (m,P)=1\\end{subarray}}g(m)\\\\\nL159: \\ll(\\log x)^{C}\\Big(\\sum_{e_{1}:\\;p_{1}^{e_{1}}\\leq x}1\\Big)\\cdots\\Big(\\sum_{e_{\\ell}:\\;p_{\\ell}^{e_{\\ell}}\\leq x}1\\Big)\\ll(\\log x)^{C+\\ell}.$$  |\nL160: \nL161: This proves the first estimate. For the second, we have\nL162: \nL163:  | $$\\sum_{n\\leq x}h(n)\\leq x\\sum_{n\\leq x}g(n),$$  |\nL164: \nL165: which implies the claim. ∎\nL166: Regarding the level of distribution of shifted primes, one expects more can be said:\nL167: ###### Conjecture 5.\nL168: \nL169: The shifted primes have level of distribution $\\vartheta=1$.\nL170: \nL171: Indeed this is a slightly weaker version of the Elliott-Halberstam conjecture [cite39†6 , Ch. 9.2].\nL172: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Non-abelian Large Sieve and Artin’s primitive root conjecture (https://arxiv.org/html/2608.21573v1)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2608.21573v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 631\n"
